$DIR/main.p:5:22: main.(*O).Drop keeps o past the call, but the owner it points to is destroyed when Drop returns
    A Drop method's receiver points to the owner being dropped: its fields are
    dropped and its memory released when Drop returns (language spec section
    18.6, What dropping does). Keeping o, or a part of the owner, in package
    memory, the heap, a channel or a task leaves a view of memory that is gone.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Copy the values Drop needs to keep out of the owner, rather than keeping a
    pointer into it
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:7:27: cannot pass &mk() to (*O).Drop (called when mk() is dropped), which keeps it after returning
    &mk() points into the temporary O that mk().n returns. The O made at
    main.p:7 is a temporary: nothing keeps it, so its object is dropped at the
    end of the statement. A view of the object may be passed to calls in that
    statement but may not outlive it. (*O).Drop (called when mk() is dropped)
    stores o in package variable keep (main.p:5), so &mk() would outlive the
    temporary.
    The view does not own the memory it looks at. Nothing keeps the temporary
    after the statement, so a stored view would outlive the value that keeps its
    memory alive.
    Change (*O).Drop (called when mk() is dropped) to take a value or an owner,
    which it can keep: an owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a library owner
    such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3009" for a longer explanation)
